<ul class="enc-list"><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/addanki"><span class="enc-name">Addanki</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Addanki is a town in Prakasam district, Andhra Pradesh, India.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/addedomarus"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/addedomarus.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Addedomarus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Addedomarus was a British king known from his coins, who ruled the Trinovantes in what is now Essex in the late 1st century BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/addis-ababa"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/addis-ababa.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Addis Ababa</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Addis Ababa is the capital of Ethiopia, founded in the late nineteenth century under Emperor Menelik II.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/addizione-erculea"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/addizione-erculea.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Addizione Erculea</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Addizione Erculea was a planned extension of Ferrara begun in 1492 for Duke Ercole I d'Este and designed by the architect Biagio Rossetti.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/addu-atoll"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/addu-atoll.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Addu Atoll</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Addu Atoll is the southernmost atoll of the Maldives, just south of the equator, and home to the country's second city.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/adel-shah"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/adel-shah.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Adel Shah</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Adel Shah, born Ali Qoli Khan, was a nephew of Nader Shah who took part in the conspiracy against him and became shah of the Afsharid Empire after Nader's murder in 1747.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/adela-of-normandy"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/adela-of-normandy.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Adela of Normandy</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Adela of Normandy was a daughter of William the Conqueror who married Stephen-Henry, Count of Blois, and governed his lands while he was on the First Crusade and after…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/adelaide"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/adelaide.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Adelaide</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Adelaide is the capital of South Australia, on the plains between Gulf St Vincent and the Mount Lofty Ranges.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/adelaide-del-vasto"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/adelaide-del-vasto.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Adelaide del Vasto</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Adelaide del Vasto was the third wife of Roger I, Count of Sicily, and regent of the County of Sicily for her young sons from 1101 to 1112.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/adelaide-of-italy"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/adelaide-of-italy.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Adelaide of Italy</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Adelaide of Italy was the daughter of King Rudolf II of Burgundy and became queen of Italy by her first marriage to Lothair II.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/adelaide-of-saxe-meiningen"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/adelaide-of-saxe-meiningen.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Adelaide of Saxe-Meiningen</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Adelaide of Saxe-Meiningen was queen consort of the United Kingdom and Hanover from 1830 to 1837 as the wife of William IV.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/adelaide-of-susa"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/adelaide-of-susa.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Adelaide of Susa</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Adelaide of Susa was the heiress of the March of Turin and one of the most powerful rulers in 11th-century Italy.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/adelaide-countess-of-vermandois"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/adelaide-countess-of-vermandois.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Adelaide, Countess of Vermandois</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Adelaide was Countess of Vermandois and Valois, heiress of the Herbertian counts of Vermandois, a branch of the Carolingian dynasty.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/adelasia-of-torres"><span class="enc-name">Adelasia of Torres</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Adelasia of Torres was judge, or ruler, of the Judicate of Logudoro in northern Sardinia from 1236 until her death in 1259.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/adelindis-of-buchau"><span class="enc-name">Adelindis of Buchau</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Adelindis was a Frankish noblewoman who, according to tradition, founded the convent of Buchau on the Federsee in upper Swabia around 770 and became its first abbess.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/aden"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/aden.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Aden</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Aden is a port city in southern Yemen, built in the crater of an extinct volcano near the entrance to the Red Sea.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/aden-abdullah-osman"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/aden-abdullah-osman.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Aden Abdullah Osman</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Aden Abdullah Osman, known as Aden Adde, was the first president of Somalia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/aden-emergency"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/aden-emergency.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Aden Emergency</span><span class="enc-type">Revolt · 1963–1967</span><span class="enc-snip">The Aden Emergency was an insurgency against British rule in Aden and the Federation of South Arabia, lasting from 1963 to 1967.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/aden-protectorate"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/aden-protectorate.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Aden Protectorate</span><span class="enc-type">State</span><span class="enc-snip">The Aden Protectorate was the group of sultanates, emirates and sheikhdoms in southern Arabia bound to Britain by treaties of protection around the port of Aden.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/aden-trades-union-congress"><span class="enc-name">Aden Trades Union Congress</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">The Aden Trades Union Congress was the federation of trade unions in the British colony of Aden, founded in 1956 amid the rapid growth of the port and the oil refinery.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/adham-khan"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/adham-khan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Adham Khan</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Adham Khan was a Mughal general, son of the emperor Akbar's wet-nurse Maham Anga.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/adhemar-of-le-puy"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/adhemar-of-le-puy.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Adhemar of Le Puy</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Adhemar of Le Puy, also called Adhemar of Monteil, was bishop of Le Puy and the papal legate appointed by Pope Urban II to the First Crusade.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/adherbal"><span class="enc-name">Adherbal</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Adherbal was a king of Numidia, the elder son of Micipsa, who shared the kingdom from 118 BCE with his brother Hiempsal and his adoptive brother Jugurtha.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/adiatuanus"><span class="enc-name">Adiatuanus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Adiatuanus was king of the Sotiates, an Aquitanian people of south-western Gaul, in 56 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/adib-shishakli"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/adib-shishakli.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Adib Shishakli</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Adib Shishakli was a Syrian army officer who took part in the coups of 1949 and ruled Syria as its military strongman from late 1949 to 1954, as president from 1953.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/al-adid"><span class="enc-name">al-Adid</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Al-Adid was the last caliph of the Fatimid Caliphate, who died in 1171.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/adige"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/adige.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Adige</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Adige is the second-longest river in Italy, about 410 kilometres long.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/adikavi-pampa"><span class="enc-name">Adikavi Pampa</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Pampa, called Adikavi or 'first poet', was a 10th-century Kannada poet at the court of the Vemulavada Chalukya king Arikesari II.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/al-adil"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/al-adil.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">al-Adil</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Al-Adil was the brother of Saladin and sultan of the Ayyubid realm from 1200 to 1218.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/adil-shah"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/adil-shah.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Adil Shah</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Adil Shah was a nephew of Nader Shah who seized power in the Afsharid Empire after Nader's assassination in 1747.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/adil-shahi-dynasty"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/adil-shahi-dynasty.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Adil Shahi dynasty</span><s