="enc-name">Adolf Schulten</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Adolf Schulten was a German historian and archaeologist of ancient Spain.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/adolf-william-duke-of-saxe-eisenach"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/adolf-william-duke-of-saxe-eisenach.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Adolf William, Duke of Saxe-Eisenach</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Adolf William was a son of Duke William of Saxe-Weimar who received Eisenach as his own duchy when his father's lands were divided in 1662.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/adolfo-lopez-mateos"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/adolfo-lopez-mateos.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Adolfo López Mateos</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Adolfo López Mateos was president of Mexico from 1958 to 1964 for the Institutional Revolutionary Party.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/adolfo-suarez"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/adolfo-suarez.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Adolfo Suárez</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Adolfo Suárez was a Spanish politician who as prime minister from 1976 to 1981 led the transition from Franco's dictatorship to democracy.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/adolo"><span class="enc-name">Adolo</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Adolo was an Oba, or king, of the Benin Empire in the nineteenth century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/adolphe-sax"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/adolphe-sax.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Adolphe Sax</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Adolphe Sax was a Belgian instrument maker, born in Dinant, who invented the saxophone, patented in Paris in 1846.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/adolphe-thiers"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/adolphe-thiers.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Adolphe Thiers</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Adolphe Thiers was a French historian and statesman who served as a minister and prime minister under the July Monarchy.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/adolphe-grand-duke-of-luxembourg"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/adolphe-grand-duke-of-luxembourg.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Adolphe, Grand Duke of Luxembourg</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Adolphe was the last Duke of Nassau, from 1839 to 1866, and Grand Duke of Luxembourg from 1890 to 1905.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/adolphus-frederick-iii-duke-of-mecklenburg-strelitz"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/adolphus-frederick-iii-duke-of-mecklenburg-strelitz.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Adolphus Frederick III, Duke of Mecklenburg-Strelitz</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Adolphus Frederick III (1686–1752) was Duke of Mecklenburg-Strelitz from 1708, the second ruler of the duchy created by the partition of Mecklenburg in 1701.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/adolphus-frederick-vi-grand-duke-of-mecklenburg-strelitz"><span class="enc-name">Adolphus Frederick VI, Grand Duke of Mecklenburg-Strelitz</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Adolphus Frederick VI was the last reigning Grand Duke of Mecklenburg-Strelitz, from 1914 to 1918.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/adomnan"><span class="enc-name">Adomnán</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Adomnán (c. 624–704) was abbot of Iona from 679 and the biographer of its founder, Columba.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/adonijah"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/adonijah.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Adonijah</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Adonijah was the fourth son of King David according to the Hebrew Bible.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/adonis"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/adonis.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Adonis</span><span class="enc-type">Religion / idea</span><span class="enc-snip">Adonis was a figure of Greek myth, a beautiful youth loved by Aphrodite who was killed by a boar and mourned each year in the festival of the Adonia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/adoptionism"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/adoptionism.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Adoptionism</span><span class="enc-type">Religion / idea</span><span class="enc-snip">Adoptionism is the Christian doctrine that Jesus, in his human nature, was the adopted rather than the natural Son of God.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/adour"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/adour.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Adour</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Adour is a river of south-western France, about 300 km long, rising in the Pyrenees near the Tourmalet pass and flowing through Bigorre, the Landes and Dax to the…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/adovacrius"><span class="enc-name">Adovacrius</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Adovacrius was a Saxon leader who fought in north-western Gaul in the 460s.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/adrar-des-ifoghas"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/adrar-des-ifoghas.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Adrar des Ifoghas</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Adrar des Ifoghas is a sandstone massif in the Kidal region of north-eastern Mali, in the southern Sahara.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/adriaan-van-jaarsveld"><span class="enc-name">Adriaan van Jaarsveld</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Adriaan van Jaarsveld was a frontier farmer and commando leader on the eastern border of the Dutch Cape Colony, known for campaigns against the Xhosa.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/adrian-pelt"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/adrian-pelt.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Adrian Pelt</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Adrian Pelt was a Dutch journalist and international civil servant who served as United Nations Commissioner in Libya from 1950 to 1951.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/adrian-von-bubenberg"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/adrian-von-bubenberg.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Adrian von Bubenberg</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Adrian von Bubenberg was a Bernese knight and statesman, several times Schultheiss (mayor) of Bern.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/adud-al-dawla"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/adud-al-dawla.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Adud al-Dawla</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Adud al-Dawla was the greatest ruler of the Buyid dynasty.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/adulis"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/adulis.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Adulis</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Adulis was an ancient port on the Red Sea coast of present-day Eritrea.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/adur-gushnasp"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/adur-gushnasp.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Adur Gushnasp</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">Adur Gushnasp was one of the three great sacred fires of Zoroastrian Iran, associated with the warrior class and the kings.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/advance-australia-fair"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/advance-australia-fair.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Advance Australia Fair</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">Advance Australia Fair is the national anthem of Australia, written by Peter Dodds McCormick and first performed in Sydney in 1878.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/advocate-of-the-holy-sepulchre"><span class="enc-name">Advocate of the Holy Sepulchre</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">Advocate of the Holy Sepulchre was the title taken by Godfrey of Bouillon after the crusaders captured Jerusalem in 1099, when he declined to be called king.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/adygea"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/adygea.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Adygea</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Adygea is a republic of the Russian Federation in the North Caucasus, surrounded by Krasnodar Krai, with its capital at Maykop.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/adyghe-khabze"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/adyghe-khabze.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Adyghe Khabze</span><span class="enc-type">Religion / idea</span><span class="enc-snip">Adyghe Khabze is the traditional unwritten code of conduct and customary law of the Circassians.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/aeacidae"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/aeacidae.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Aeacidae</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Aeacidae were the royal house of the Molossians and the Kingdom of Epirus, who claimed descent from Neoptolemus, son of Achilles and grandson of Aeacus.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/aeclanum"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/aeclanum.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Aeclanum</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Aeclanum was the main town of the Hirpini in the southern Apennines, near modern Mirabella Eclano in Campania.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/aed-find"><span class="enc-name">Áed Find</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Áed Find was a king of Dál Riata of the Cenél nGabráin, whose death the Irish annals record in 778.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/aed-findliath"><span class="enc-name">Áed Findliath</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Áed Findliath was king of Ailech, of the northern Uí Néill, and High King of Ireland from 862 to 879.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/aed-mac-ainmuirech"><span class="enc-name">Áed 