ia/aegean"><span class="enc-name">Aegean</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Aegean is the arm of the Mediterranean Sea between Greece and Anatolia, scattered with numerous islands.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/aegidius"><span class="enc-name">Aegidius</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Aegidius was a Roman general who commanded in northern Gaul from about 457 until his death in 464 or 465.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/aegitna"><span class="enc-name">Aegitna</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Aegitna was an ancient port on the coast of southern Gaul, in the territory of the Ligurian Oxybii, known chiefly from the historian Polybius.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/aelia-capitolina"><span class="enc-name">Aelia Capitolina</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Aelia Capitolina was the Roman colony founded by the emperor Hadrian on the ruins of Jerusalem around 130 CE, with a temple to Jupiter Capitolinus.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/aelia-eudoxia"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/aelia-eudoxia.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Aelia Eudoxia</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Aelia Eudoxia (died 404) was the wife of the eastern Roman emperor Arcadius, whom she married in 395.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/aelius-gallus"><span class="enc-name">Aelius Gallus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Aelius Gallus was the second Roman prefect of Egypt, appointed by Augustus.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/aeneas"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/aeneas.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Aeneas</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Aeneas is a Trojan hero of Greek and Roman legend, son of the prince Anchises and the goddess Venus (Aphrodite).</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/aeneid"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/aeneid.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Aeneid</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Aeneid is a Latin epic poem in twelve books by Virgil, written between about 29 and 19 BCE.</span></a></li></ul>
